    "q-119559": {
      "dom_id": "step-q119559",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Determine the value of $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}-3\\,\\frac{1}{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(3\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{13}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{4}-\\frac{13}{4}\n$$\n\nSince both fractions have the same denominators, we can combine them into one fraction, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{4}-\\frac{13}{4}=\\frac{11-13}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $11-13$ using a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14148354/assets/befeb1f1947d5325530ac772fcf1411b04ae74bd78e377dc3eff547461a87627.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{11-13}{4}=\\frac{-2}{4}=-\\frac{2}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{4}=-\\frac{2÷2}{4÷2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
