    "q-119396": {
      "dom_id": "step-q119396",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Calculate the value of $-\\frac{2}{3}-1\\frac{5}{6}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{(1\\times 6)+5}{6}=\\frac{11}{6}\n$$\n\nAlso, we write $-\\frac{2}{3}$ as $-\\frac{4}{6}$ and then we simply subtract the numerators. This gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{6}-\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{-4-11}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $-4-11$ using a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14148354/assets/69aec22c2950768abf58a2cae84d3b90a52c79ad44e60d0008605feca272357e.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{-4-11}{6}=\\frac{-15}{6}=-\\frac{15}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{15}{6}=-\\frac{15÷3}{6÷3}=-\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{2}=-2\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
